{"name":"Pierogi","hex":"#f4d68c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pierogi","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A square- or crescent-shaped dumpling of unleavened dough, stuffed with sauerkraut, cheese, mashed potatoes, cabbage, onion, meat, or any combination of these, or with a fruit filling.","↑ Mitchell Davis (2002), “Pierogi”, in The Mensch Chef or Why Delicious Jewish Food Isn’t an Oxymoron, New York, N.Y.: Clarkson Potter/Publishers, →ISBN, chapter 1 (Dinner Starts at Five: Appetizers and Salads), page 18: “Just in case you were wondering, pierogi is a Polish plural. Pierogis is grammatically incorrect. One is simply called a pierog.”"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pierogi"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pierogi","blocks":[{"text":"Pierogi are filled dumplings made by wrapping unleavened dough around a filling and cooking in boiling water. They are occasionally flavored with a savory or sweet garnish. Typical fillings include potato and tvorog cheese, sauerkraut, ground meat, mushrooms, fruits, or berries. Savory pierogi are often served with a topping of sour cream, fried onions, or both."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Pierogi_z_mas%C5%82em_-_2023.03.31.jpg/330px-Pierogi_z_mas%C5%82em_-_2023.03.31.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierogi","wikidata":"Q697800"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/04565_Christmas_dumplings_with_dried_plums.JPG/500px-04565_Christmas_dumplings_with_dried_plums.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/04565_Christmas_dumplings_with_dried_plums.JPG/500px-04565_Christmas_dumplings_with_dried_plums.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Traditional Christmas Eve pierogi, whose name is derive